 <div class="stretch-content"><p>1) Improve: 'The source proves the port was busy.'</p><div class='print-line'></div><p>2) Sketch the support spectrum in four steps.</p><div class='print-line'></div><p>3) A source supports a claim for 1907 only. What's the limit called?</p><div class='print-line'></div><p>4) GCSE stem: 'How far does Source A support…' — what's demanded?</p><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-starter" class="print-section"><h2>Starter: The Claim on Trial</h2><p><strong>Key Question:</strong> Claim: 'Middlesbrough's dock was thriving in the 1900s.' Exhibit A: one photograph, one morning, 1907. How far can one frozen moment carry that claim?</p><div class="print-line"></div><div class="print-line"></div></div><div id="print-wedo" class="print-section prevent-break"><h2>We Do 1: Where on the Spectrum?</h2><p>Groups: ✅ SUPPORTS STRONGLY, 🟡 PARTLY / LIMITED, ❌ UNDERMINES. Sort: Port ledger: rising tonnage 1901–09, The 1907 photo — one busy morning, 1908 strike report: quay idle six weeks, Postcard captioned 'our bustling dock', Harbour dues receipts climbing yearly, Trade directory: two shippers folded 1905, A docker's memoir, written 1950, New crane investment minuted 1906</p>
